    <desc>Copy and paste text, also with its colors, and paste file names.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Copy and paste</title>

<p>You can copy and paste text in <app>Pano Terminal</app> in a similar way
  to other applications. However, the keyboard shortcuts differ.</p>

  <terms>
    <item>
      <title>Copy</title>
      <p>Select the text you wish to copy, then right-click it and select
      <gui style="menuitem">Copy</gui>, or press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>C</key></keyseq>.
      A short notice confirms it; to turn it off, open the
      <gui>Preferences</gui> and turn off <gui>Confirm Copying</gui> in the
      <gui>Behavior</gui> group.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Copy as HTML</title>
      <p>Select the text you wish to copy with its colors and font attributes,
      then right-click it and select
      <gui style="menuitem">Copy as HTML</gui>.</p>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Paste</title>
      <p>Right-click in the terminal and select
      <gui style="menuitem">Paste</gui>, or press <keyseq><key>Shift</key><key>Ctrl</key><key>V</key></keyseq>.
      If you copied files in the file manager,
      <gui style="menuitem">Paste as Filenames</gui> pastes their paths,
      quoted for the shell.</p>
    </item>
  </terms>

  <p>Selected text can also be pasted with a middle click, in the terminal or
  in another application.</p>

  <note style="tip">
    <p>The standard keyboard shortcuts, such as <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>C</key></keyseq>, cannot be
    used to copy and paste text, as programs in the terminal use them.</p>
  </note>

  <p>To copy the output of a command, see
  <link xref="cmd-shell-integration#copy-output"/>. To copy URLs, paths and
  other text without the mouse, see <link xref="txt-quick-select"/>.</p>

